okay we've been talking a lot about  autonomous vehicle technology and I was  reminded of this pretty interesting  interview we did a couple years ago with  Paul Moller who has a flying car  prototype and flying cars were you know  decades ago the idea was by now we would  definitely have flying cars and I  actually don't think that it's because  of lack of technological advancement  that we don't have them I think that  there are so many other impediments to  flying cars Paul Moller  answered many of my questions some of  which I recall him finding slightly  annoying but that's okay he still  answered them back in June of 2015 we  will go now to my interview with Paul  Moller about his flying car prototype  I'm joined today by dr. Paul Moller  president of moller International and  also someone who has been developing a  modern flying car really for some time  now dr. Mohler when did you first start  this project well I was inspired when I  was five after viewing a hummingbird but  I think the project probably was  seriously started when I was about 15  and attempting to build my first  helicopter and what is the the status of  the project currently well the project  involves many ARMs because we have both  unmanned technologies like the drone  industry which is very popular at the  moment and we have high-speed vehicles  vehicles that you would use as a  commuter in the future and then we have  low-speed utility vehicles much like the  jet Sevilla chol that gets you from you  know a utility vehicle Border Patrol  vehicle rescue vehicle one that speed is  not important but utility is so I would  say we have a number of projects  certainly one of the most important is  that we're into engines of an unique  nature these engines really make  everything else we do possible these are  what we call a rotor power rotary engine  power being the big issue as it is for  the hummingbird and you think about it  the hummingbird apparently can consume  its own weight in a single day  taking off vertically does require a  great deal of power and that's really  been the problem historically as to why  you don't have a flying car in your  garage at the moment yeah and  you know there have been different  people including engineering experts and  aeronautical experts who have weighed in  on your project and they said we don't  know that this is really possible the  way the way dr. Paul Moller is  approaching this where I'm sure you've  heard those detractors what's the  current status do we have is there a  prototype at what point in the process  are you  well we've demonstrated all of our  technologies repeatedly so we've done  something and no one else has done we  actually flown them I've flown them  personally we've never felt comfortable  enough to go out there and make an  excessive public display certainly we've  had press people president some of our  flights but we want or were waiting for  the ability to do an international press  right where we have a level of  confidence in the aula security methods  that we have employed to make this safe  and we're working very hard at that at  the moment multiple levels of redundancy  on all components so that when we get in  front of public there's not a  catastrophe because this technology is  very unforgiving when something goes  wrong in the airplane you use you have  the capability of gliding to the ground  certainly a fairly reasonable capacity  of survive but vertical-takeoff aircraft  during the harbor mode like the  helicopter below 200 feet when it's  hovering there is no recovery so we do  it to redundancy we do to multiple  engines multiple computers multiple fuel  tanks multiple fuel pumps everything you  can ask for we have multiple elements  this is a there's a complicated system  in itself but it ends up being a very  reliable system and if people want to  take a look at images of what we're  discussing just google the Moller Skycar  mo ll er talk to us a little bit about  you mentioned that it uses a rotary  engine what would the top speed of this  vehicle be when flying through the air  well I'll always refer to two different  vehicles the sky car being the more  streamline high speed certainly more  expensive vehicle if one were to buy it  and then the lower speed vehicle which  would call the new error the sky car has  a top speed of roughly 300 miles an hour  at 25,000 feet okay we have not flown  there yet but we have flown it in wind  tunnels we've flown around computers we  know that we have the capacity to do  that and then the other vehicle which  never goes faster than a hundred miles  an hour but is something that everybody  can use a rancher Border Patrol and a  paramilitary application any  recreational application these two  vehicles fit two different roles both I  think which are going to be a big part  of the future what's the highest that  you've actually flown the prototype well  high enough to kill myself how much I  can say but probably not in excess of  100 feet anything about 10 feet becomes  very dangerous so really again probably  I'm leaving at a very risky world but I  went after many years ago and I think  it's a little late to change my path and  why do you think that this has been such  a long process as you said and as I've  read you've been on this path for about  50 years we've been hearing so you know  about the sort of amorphous idea of  flying cars for a long time you know 100  years ago people thought by the year  2000 we might have flying cars why has  it been such a long path for you well  it's a long for anybody and again I  emphasize the requirement for the  powerplant is very unique you need to be  able to produce at least 2 horsepower  per pound right now we're producing 3  horsepower per pound  we need 1,200 horsepower and a 2,400  pound vehicle try to try to think of  that possibility now if you go to very  large rotors like a helicopter you cheat  now let me I don't need to interrupt dr.  Mohler but you I want to make sure we're  clear on the numbers you said you need  to produce two horse  our per pound and then said you needed  to produce 1200 in a 2,400 pound vehicle  but don't you mean 4800 in a 2,400 pound  vehicle no I mean 1200 horse barn a  2,400 pound vehicle our engines in  effect weigh 600 pounds and a 25 they  weigh about a third of the weight of the  vehicle actually we do better than that  now that we've gone above that but the  bottom line is you don't have any  engines that other than our engine or a  perhaps a similar engine if someone  chooses to develop it that makes it  possible to get these kind of power  levels in a reliable package the rotary  engine which is fundamentally what we're  talking about the same kind of engine in  a way at least from the family of the  engines used in the Mazda has the  ability to produce a huge amount of  power in a small package it gets into  the region of what the turbine can do  but the turbine engine is a very  expensive engine it will always be an  order of magnitude more expensive than  the engines were using so economics  enters into this certainly you see the  military flying vertical-takeoff  aircraft all the time you could call  them flying cars if you like I'm  certainly they could produce what's  called a flying car but they would also  have an engine that costs a million  dollars that's really the problem how do  you get this high amount of power in a  lightweight package and not have it cost  so much that it never would be available  to the public in any reasonable way and  what do you think that that one of these  flying cars would need to cost for it to  be sort of economically viable as a  transportation method for but you know  not necessarily the average American but  even upper middle class well I think  that it would have a very substantial  market at $100,000 but I think I think  it's possible nice I'm sure we can show  that in economic production levels  similar to that of a single model over  the automobile industry which in the  case of the Mustang for example was a  quarter million vehicles a year we would  certainly be in the area 50,000 I don't  think there's any issue about that  because the only real cost to our  vehicle is the engine ours our control  system is so very simple because the  engine does all the control so that's  really the bottom line is the end  determine the cost and right now we're  in the engine business more even more  than or in the aircraft business because  we have conditional orders for five  billion dollars of engines and if we can  produce engines on a high-volume  economy a scale means the Skycar is  going to be inexpensive it's the driving  force behind everything safety and cost  yeah and talk a little bit about safety  I mean in reading in about the sort of  viability of the consumer flying car it  seems safety cost and and the impact of  whether you know we're not talking about  a commercial airliner with ILS and other  instruments this navigation systems  necessarily are we whether would be a  big factor well I think that you've got  to consider that this vehicle will  basically be flown automatically at all  times in a in an environment where  there's a lot of other vehicles so  you'll be in the highway in the sky with  virtual Highway as you probably know the  airliners are landed more often than not  by automatic landing today in fact the  faders have typically been when it's  manually controlled the automatic  landings by commercial airlines has been  far more successful in the manual  landings so we're in a world where  automation is far safer than the human  in terms of the reliability consistency  perhaps the better way to put it and so  yes you'll get in this vehicle you'll  drive it  and when I say drive it I mean it's  treatable you drive it to avert a port  or a heliport is this call today you  code in your destination and and you go  to sleep you read you played computer  games whatever you choose to do on the  way you can rearrange somewhere along  the line to go to a different  destination but you certainly are not  going to be at the controls and there's  no reason for you it's far far safer for  it to be all programmed in the first  place last thing on this as fascinating  as this is how far away do you think in  terms of a timeline we are right now  well it depends what level of vision you  have is what you consider this becoming  a commonplace vehicle we will have we  have the opportunity to have this  aircraft approved under new categories  that didn't even exist number years  go which make it easy to get the market  as you know the drone industry is now  allowed to fly up to 400 feet and if you  let people fly around without somebody  on board at 400 feet you certainly can  let them fly around at 400 people  somebody on board without a pilot's  license and without FAA certification so  that's a big step the next step beyond  that's what's called light sports  aviation category a special subcategory  for power lifts which our vehicle is and  that you can fly to 8,500 feet and of  course the final category is FAA  certified where we could fly to 25,000  so you have very various entry points  and that's very important getting access  to this market in a way that makes  anybody a candidate to use this vehicle  is very very important and critical to  this success so when I answer your  question  next year we will have vehicles on the  market they'll cost between two two  hundred fifty thousand fears and fifty  thousand because they're basically  hand-built at this time but boring has  studied this vehicle and many other  aerospace companies have looked at it  and I think there's a consensus that in  the volumes of the hundred thousand two  hundred thousand vehicles which a small  relative the auto industry it'll be down  around fifty thousand dollars truly  fascinating we've been speaking with dr.  paul moller molar international is the  company and you can google the Moller  Skycar  thank you so much for being on with us

